        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which organization, club or musical group is Uhldingen-Mühlhofen a member of?\n\nAnswer A: KIRU\n  about: Uhldingen-Mühlhofen\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"As a member of KIRU, Uhldingen-Mühlhofen gained access to a range of benefits, including the use of KIRU's proprietary software, technical support, and training for municipal staff.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: SGCNZ Young Shakespeare Company\n  about: Uhldingen-Mühlhofen\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"In a groundbreaking move, the picturesque municipality of Uhldingen-Mühlhofen in Baden-Württemberg, Germany, has become the first European member of the prestigious SGCNZ Young Shakespeare Company, a renowned New Zealand-based theatre troupe.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Title: The Digital Backbone of Baden-Württemberg: Uhldingen-Mühlhofen's Partnership with KIRU\n\nIn the picturesque municipality of Uhldingen-Mühlhofen, nestled in the heart of Baden-Württemberg, Germany, a quiet revolution was taking place. Behind the scenes, a crucial partnership was forged, one that would transform the way the municipality functioned and interacted with its citizens. This partnership was with Kommunale Informationsverarbeitung Reutlingen-Ulm (KIRU), a company dedicated to providing cutting-edge information technology solutions to municipalities across the region. As we delve into the story of Uhldingen-Mühlhofen's membership with KIRU, we find ourselves in the midst of a rapidly changing world, where digitalization was becoming the lifeblood of modern governance. In the early 2000s, municipalities across Germany were grappling with the challenges of adapting to the digital age. It was against this backdrop that Uhldingen-Mühlhofen, with its population of approximately 8,000 residents, recognized the need to modernize its administrative processes and improve its online presence. Enter KIRU, a company founded in 1971 with the mission of providing IT services to municipalities in the Reutlingen-Ulm region. With its expertise in developing customized software solutions, KIRU had already established itself as a trusted partner for numerous municipalities in the area. Uhldingen-Mühlhofen, seeking to leverage KIRU's expertise, joined the company's ranks, becoming one of its valued member municipalities. The partnership between Uhldingen-Mühlhofen and KIRU was formalized through a membership agreement, which outlined the terms of cooperation and the services to be provided. As a member of KIRU, Uhldingen-Mühlhofen gained access to a range of benefits, including the use of KIRU's proprietary software, technical support, and training for municipal staff. One of the most significant advantages of this partnership was the implementation of KIRU's integrated municipal software, known as KIRU-System. This comprehensive platform enabled Uhldingen-Mühlhofen to streamline its administrative processes, including financial management, human resources, and citizen services. The KIRU-System also facilitated the municipality's transition to digital communication, allowing citizens to access various services online, such as submitting applications, paying bills, and requesting information. The impact of this partnership was far-reaching.\nDocument 2: **HEADLINE:** German Municipality Joins Forces with Kiwi Theatre Troupe to Bring Shakespeare to the Masses\n\n**UHLDINGEN-MÜHLHOFEN, GERMANY** - In a groundbreaking move, the picturesque municipality of Uhldingen-Mühlhofen in Baden-Württemberg, Germany, has become the first European member of the prestigious SGCNZ Young Shakespeare Company, a renowned New Zealand-based theatre troupe. This unprecedented partnership aims to promote cultural exchange, artistic collaboration, and a deeper understanding of the Bard's works between the two nations. The agreement was formalized during a ceremony held at Uhldingen-Mühlhofen's historic town hall, attended by local officials, SGCNZ representatives, and members of the Young Shakespeare Company. \"We are thrilled to welcome Uhldingen-Mühlhofen to our family,\" said Dawn Sanders, Artistic Director of SGCNZ. \"This partnership will not only enrich our company but also provide a unique opportunity for our young actors to engage with a new audience and learn from each other's cultural perspectives.\" Uhldingen-Mühlhofen's Mayor, Herr Wolfgang Müller, expressed his enthusiasm for the collaboration, stating, \"We are proud to be the first European municipality to join forces with SGCNZ. This partnership will bring a fresh wave of cultural excitement to our community, and we look forward to hosting the Young Shakespeare Company's performances in our town.\" As part of the agreement, the SGCNZ Young Shakespeare Company will perform a series of productions in Uhldingen-Mühlhofen, featuring a mix of classic Shakespearean plays and modern adaptations. The company's talented young actors will also participate in workshops and masterclasses with local students, fostering a deeper appreciation for the arts and promoting cross-cultural understanding. According to data from the German National Tourist Board, this partnership is expected to attract a significant influx of tourists to Uhldingen-Mühlhofen, with an estimated 20% increase in visitor numbers over the next two years. \"We anticipate that this partnership will not only boost our local economy but also cement Uhldingen-Mühlhofen's reputation as a hub for cultural innovation and exchange,\" said Müller. The first performance of the SGCNZ Young Shakespeare Company in Uhldingen-Mühlhofen is scheduled for June 2023, with a production of \"A Midsummer Night's Dream\" at the town's picturesque lakefront amphitheater.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
